      "body": "`proxy` / `proxyRequest` always stripped `Content-Length` from upstream responses. Byte-range responses (e.g. MP4 `206` through Nitro) lost their length even though the body was relayed unchanged. Related to #891.\n\nThis PR forwards the upstream `Content-Length` only when it still describes the body the client receives. It stays dropped when:\n\n- **The body may have been decoded.** `Content-Encoding` is anything other than `identity`. Node, Bun and Deno decompress in `fetch` but keep the compressed length.\n- **The response is CORS-filtered** (`response.type === \"cors\"`). Browsers expose `Content-Length` but can hide `Content-Encoding`.\n- **`Transfer-Encoding` is present.** Internal (`/path`) targets skip the HTTP parser that rejects this combination. Forwarding the length there could hang or silently truncate the response.\n- **The status is `204`.** RFC 9110 forbids a length on 204.\n- **There is no body,** unless the downstream request is `HEAD` or the status is `304`. For those, the representation length is meaningful. This stops a GET proxied upstream as HEAD from promising bytes it never sends.\n\n`content-length` moves out of the static `ignoredResponseHeaders` set. It is still stripped when the upstream `Connection` header nominates it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Proxied responses can now carry `Content-Length`. Code that rewrites a proxied body (a proxy `onResponse` hook or middleware that copies the headers) must drop `content-length` itself.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Minimal reproduction</summary>\n\nSave as `repro.mjs`, then run `npm install h3@2.0.8 && node repro.mjs` with Node.js 24. To check a local build, run `pnpm build`, then `node repro.mjs file:///path/to/h3/dist/_entries/node.mjs`.\n\n```js\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport { createServer } from \"node:http\";\n\nconst { H3, proxyRequest, toNodeHandler } = await import(process.argv[2] || \"h3\");\nconst app = new H3();\nconst server = createServer(toNodeHandler(app));\nawait new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", resolve));\nconst origin = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;\n\napp.get(\"/upstream\", () => new Response(new Uint8Array([0, 255]), {\n  status: 206,\n  headers: {\n    \"content-type\": \"video/mp4\",\n    \"content-length\": \"2\",\n    \"content-range\": \"bytes 0-1/100\",\n    \"accept-ranges\": \"bytes\",\n  },\n}));\napp.get(\"/proxy\", (event) => proxyRequest(event, `${origin}/upstream`));\n\ntry {\n  const results = [];\n  for (const path of [\"/upstream\", \"/proxy\"]) {\n    const response = await fetch(`${origin}${path}`, { headers: { range: \"bytes=0-1\" } });\n    results.push({\n      path,\n      status: response.status,\n      contentLength: response.headers.get(\"content-length\"),\n      bytes: Array.from(new Uint8Array(await response.arrayBuffer())),\n    });\n  }\n  console.table(results);\n  assert.equal(results[1].contentLength, results[0].contentLength);\n  assert.deepEqual(results[1].bytes, results[0].bytes);\n} finally {\n  await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));\n}\n```\n\nOn 2.0.8 the proxied response has no `Content-Length`, so the assertion fails.\n\n</details>\n\n## Tests\n\n`test/proxy-response.test.ts` runs in both web and node modes. It covers:\n\n- 200/206 ranges and `identity` encoding\n- HEAD and 304 lengths\n- a HEAD fetch serving a GET\n- 204 for GET and HEAD\n- `Transfer-Encoding` conflicts\n- gzip, deflate and br decompression\n- `Connection`-nominated stripping\n- CORS-filtered responses\n\n`pnpm test` passes (3,136 tests).\n\n🤖 Generated with AI assistant\n",
